      "text": "To determine the day of the week for December 23, we need to calculate the number of days from November 23 to December 23 and then find the corresponding day of the week.\n\n1. First, calculate the number of days from November 23 to December 23.\n   November has 30 days, and since November 23 is not included in the count, we need to count the days from the 23rd to the 30th of November plus the days from the 1st of December to the 23rd of December.\n   - From November 23 to November 30: \\(30 - 23 + 1 = 8\\) days.\n   - From December 1 to December 23: \\(23 - 1 + 1 = 23\\) days.\n   - Total days from November 23 to December 23: \\(8 + 23 = 31\\) days.\n\n2. Next, determine the day of the week 31 days after a Thursday. Since there are 7 days in a week, we can find the remainder when 31 is divided by 7.\n   \\[\n   31 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ R } 3\n   \\]\n   This means 31 days is equivalent to 4 weeks and 3 extra days. So, starting from a Thursday, adding 3 days brings us to:\n   - Thursday + 1 day = Friday\n   - Friday + 1 day = Saturday\n   - Saturday + 1 day = Sunday\n\nTherefore, December 23 is on a \\(\\boxed{\\text{Sunday}}\\).\n\nA class consists of 16 girls and 10 boys. If each girl brings 2 change items and each boy brings 1 change item, how many change items are there in total?\n\nTo determine the total number of change items brought by the students, we need to calculate the contributions from both the girls and the boys separately and then sum these amounts.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of change items brought by the girls. Each girl brings 2 change items, and there are 16 girls in the class. Therefore, the total number of change items brought by the girls is:\n\\[\n16 \\text{ girls} \\times 2 \\text{ items/girl} = 32 \\text{ items}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of change items brought by the boys. Each boy brings 1 change item, and there are 10 boys in the class. Therefore, the total number of change items brought by the boys is:\n\\[\n10 \\text{ boys} \\times 1 \\text{ item/boy} = 10 \\text{ items}\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of change items brought by all the students, we add the number of items brought by the girls and the number of items brought by the boys:\n\\[\n32 \\text{ items} + 10 \\text{ items} = 42 \\text{ items}\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of change items is \\(\\boxed{42}\\).\n\nIn $\\triangle ABC$, the sides opposite to angles $A$, $B$, and $C$ are denoted as $a$, $b$, and $c$ respectively. Given $a^2 - b^2 = \\sqrt{3}bc$ and $\\sin C = 2\\sqrt{3}\\sin B$, find the measure of angle $A$.\n\nTo solve for the measure of angle \\( A \\) in \\(\\triangle ABC\\), we start by using the given equations: \\( a^2 - b^2 = \\sqrt{3}bc \\) and \\( \\sin C = 2\\sqrt{3} \\sin B \\).\n\nFirst, we use the Law of Sines, which states that \\(\\frac{a}{\\sin A} = \\frac{b}{\\sin B} = \\frac{c}{\\sin C}\\). From the given \\(\\sin C = 2\\sqrt{3} \\sin B\\), we can express \\(\\sin C\\) in terms of \\(\\sin B\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin C = 2\\sqrt{3} \\sin B\n\\]\n\nFrom the Law of Sines, we know:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{c}{\\sin C} = \\frac{b}{\\sin B} \\implies \\frac{c}{2\\sqrt{3} \\sin B} = \\frac{b}{\\sin B} \\implies c = 2\\sqrt{3}b\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( c = 2\\sqrt{3}b \\) into the equation \\( a^2 - b^2 = \\sqrt{3}bc \\):\n\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = \\sqrt{3}b(2\\sqrt{3}b) = 6b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\na^2 = b^2 + 6b^2 = 7b^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\na = b\\sqrt{7}\n\\]\n\nNow, we use the Law of Cosines to find \\(\\cos A\\):\n\n\\[\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \\cos A\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = b\\sqrt{7} \\) and \\( c = 2\\sqrt{3}b \\) into the Law of Cosines, we get:\n\n\\[\n(b\\sqrt{7})^2 = b^2 + (2\\sqrt{3}b)^2 - 2b(2\\sqrt{3}b) \\cos A\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\n\\[\n7b^2 = b^2 + 12b^2 - 4\\sqrt{3}b^2 \\cos A\n\\]\n\n\\[\n7b^2 = 13b^2 - 4\\sqrt{3}b^2 \\cos A\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms to isolate \\(\\cos A\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n7b^2 - 13b^2 = -4\\sqrt{3}b^2 \\cos A\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-6b^2 = -4\\sqrt{3}b^2 \\cos A\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by \\(-b^2\\):\n\n\\[\n6 = 4\\sqrt{3} \\cos A\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\cos A\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos A = \\frac{6}{4\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the angle \\( A \\) is:\n\n\\[\nA = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe measure of angle \\( A \\) is \\(\\boxed{30^\\circ}\\).",
